# Mobile Components

**All components are functional.** No class components.

## Themed primitives

Don't use raw `<Text>` and `<View>` for content — use the themed wrappers so light/dark works automatically:

```tsx
// src/components/themed-text.tsx
import { StyleSheet, Text, type TextProps } from 'react-native';
import { useThemeColor } from '@/hooks/use-theme-color';

type ThemedTextProps = TextProps & {
  lightColor?: string;
  darkColor?: string;
  type?: 'default' | 'title' | 'defaultSemiBold' | 'subtitle' | 'link';
};

export function ThemedText({ style, lightColor, darkColor, type = 'default', ...rest }: ThemedTextProps) {
  // Links take the theme's tint token; everything else takes the text token.
  const color = useThemeColor({ light: lightColor, dark: darkColor }, type === 'link' ? 'tint' : 'text');
  return <Text style={[{ color }, styles[type], style]} {...rest} />;
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  default:          { fontSize: 16, lineHeight: 24 },
  defaultSemiBold:  { fontSize: 16, lineHeight: 24, fontWeight: '600' },
  title:            { fontSize: 32, fontWeight: 'bold', lineHeight: 32 },
  subtitle:         { fontSize: 20, fontWeight: 'bold' },
  link:             { fontSize: 16, lineHeight: 30 },
});
```

(The Expo stub hardcodes `#0a7ea4` in the `link` style — that's just the `tint` token's light value. Reference the token instead so links follow dark mode and rebrands.)

`<ThemedView>` follows the same pattern.

**Enforce the text rule mechanically**: WHEN a themed text wrapper exists (`ThemedText`, THIS repo) → enable `react-native/no-raw-text` with `skip: ['ThemedText']` (`eslint-plugin-react-native`) so a raw string outside the wrapper is a lint error, not a review catch. This locks in token discipline AND i18n-readiness — a raw string is also an untranslatable string. The company's most disciplined app runs exactly this config; its near-zero escape-hatch count shows it holds.

## Touchables

Choose based on visual feedback:

| Primitive | Use |
|---|---|
| `Pressable` | **Default.** Custom feedback via `pressed` state. |
| `TouchableOpacity` | Quick opacity-based feedback (legacy) |
| `TouchableHighlight` | Backdrop color change (rare) |
| `TouchableWithoutFeedback` | When you genuinely don't want feedback (consider why) |

```tsx
<Pressable
  onPress={onPress}
  accessibilityRole="button"
  accessibilityLabel="Save profile"
  style={({ pressed }) => [styles.button, pressed && styles.pressed]}
>
  <ThemedText>Save</ThemedText>
</Pressable>
```

## Lists — always FlatList / SectionList / FlashList

**Never `.map()` a long list of items inside a `<ScrollView>`.** It renders all items at once and tanks performance.

```tsx
// ✅
<FlatList
  data={items}
  keyExtractor={item => item.id}
  renderItem={({ item }) => <ItemRow item={item} />}
  onEndReached={loadMore}
  onEndReachedThreshold={0.5}
  ItemSeparatorComponent={Separator}
  ListEmptyComponent={EmptyState}
/>

// ❌
<ScrollView>
  {items.map(item => <ItemRow key={item.id} item={item} />)}
</ScrollView>
```

For very large lists, use `@shopify/flash-list` v2's `FlashList` (drop-in replacement, better recycling; v2 needs no size estimates — `estimatedItemSize` is gone, and it never existed on `FlatList`).

## Image handling

Use `expo-image`, not React Native's built-in `<Image>`:

```tsx
import { Image } from 'expo-image';

<Image
  source={{ uri: avatarUrl }}
  style={styles.avatar}
  contentFit="cover"
  transition={200}
  cachePolicy="memory-disk"
  accessibilityLabel="User avatar"
/>
```

`expo-image` is faster, supports better caching, and gives you `blurhash` placeholders.

## Accessibility

Every interactive element gets:

- `accessibilityRole` (`button`, `link`, `header`, `image`, `text`, `alert`, ...)
- `accessibilityLabel` if the visible text isn't sufficient
- `accessibilityHint` for non-obvious actions
- `accessibilityState={{ disabled, selected, checked }}` for stateful controls

Decorative content gets `accessibilityElementsHidden={true}` (iOS) + `importantForAccessibility="no-hide-descendants"` (Android), or wrap in a parent that already has `accessible`.

```tsx
<Pressable
  accessibilityRole="button"
  accessibilityLabel="Delete account"
  accessibilityHint="Permanently deletes your account"
  accessibilityState={{ disabled: isSubmitting }}
  onPress={handleDelete}
>
```

## Loading states

```tsx
import { ActivityIndicator } from 'react-native';

<ActivityIndicator size="small" color={tintColor} accessibilityLabel="Loading" />
```

Inside buttons:

```tsx
<Pressable onPress={onPress} disabled={isLoading}>
  {isLoading ? <ActivityIndicator color="#fff" /> : <ThemedText>Save</ThemedText>}
</Pressable>
```

## List/data states — empty ≠ errored

A `FlatList` whose data came from a failed fetch must NOT show its `ListEmptyComponent` as if the fetch succeeded with no rows (the user reads an outage as "my data is gone"). Branch the four outcomes explicitly — `loading` → spinner; `error` → an error view with retry; empty → `ListEmptyComponent` only when the fetch SUCCEEDED; otherwise the list. Defaulting `data` to `[]` on error and relying on `ListEmptyComponent` is the violation signal.

## Hard rules

- ❌ Class components
- ❌ `<Text>` / `<View>` for content — use themed wrappers
- ❌ Rendering the empty state on a failed fetch — "couldn't load" must look different from "nothing here"
- ❌ `.map()` over long lists in `<ScrollView>` — use `FlatList`
- ❌ Built-in `<Image>` — use `expo-image`
- ❌ Missing `accessibilityRole` / `accessibilityLabel` on interactive elements
- ❌ More than one component per file
- ❌ Component files > 500 lines
